WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.459
Canada Infrastructure Bank.

00:00:02.409 --> 00:00:05.011
Thirty-five billion dollar mandate.

00:00:05.011 --> 00:00:10.340
Created in 2017 to attract private investment in public infrastructure.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:18.704
The promise was transformational: leverage federal dollars to unlock private capital for transit, broadband, clean energy, and trade corridors.

00:00:18.704 --> 00:00:26.079
The reality: years of operational delays, executive turnover, and deployment rates that fell far short of the original mandate.

00:00:26.079 --> 00:00:28.818
The Canada Infrastructure Bank model.

00:00:28.818 --> 00:00:36.909
The CIB was designed to operate as a Crown corporation using federal capital to attract private sector co-investment in infrastructure projects.

00:00:36.909 --> 00:00:42.488
The theory: for every dollar the government invests, private investors add three to five more.

00:00:42.488 --> 00:00:52.443
The bank would focus on revenue-generating projects — transit, broadband, clean energy, trade infrastructure — where user fees or revenue streams could repay private investors.

00:00:52.443 --> 00:00:54.659
Institutional analysis.

00:00:54.659 --> 00:00:59.420
The CIB experienced three CEO changes in its first seven years.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:02.749
Its initial project pipeline took years to develop.

00:01:02.749 --> 00:01:10.647
Critics argued the model was fundamentally flawed: private investors need guaranteed returns that public infrastructure can't reliably provide.

00:01:10.647 --> 00:01:14.329
Supporters argued the bank needed time to build capacity.

00:01:14.329 --> 00:01:18.488
Both sides agree the deployment rate fell short of the original mandate.

00:01:18.488 --> 00:01:20.738
The institutional pattern.

00:01:20.738 --> 00:01:26.954
The Canada Infrastructure Bank follows the same template as other institutional failures documented on this site.

00:01:26.954 --> 00:01:29.886
Create a new entity with a large mandate.

00:01:29.886 --> 00:01:32.522
Staff it with political appointees.

00:01:32.522 --> 00:01:34.704
Set ambitious targets.

00:01:34.704 --> 00:01:37.238
Deliver below expectations.

00:01:37.238 --> 00:01:39.011
Promise reform.

00:01:39.011 --> 00:01:49.863
The CIB joins Phoenix Pay, ArriveCAN, the MAID framework, and the Foreign Influence Registry as institutions that consume significant public resources while underdelivering on their stated mandates.
